Section 1: Array Advanced Methods
Task 1.1 - map vs forEach vs reduce
Findings:
map: transforms each element, returns new array, original unchanged
forEach: executes callback per element, returns undefined, for side effects only
reduce: collapses array to single value, requires accumulator and initialValue
Always provide initialValue to reduce for empty array safety
Errors made:
Assigned forEach result to variable, got undefined
Forgot initialValue in reduce, accumulator started as first element (wrong type)
Confused parameter order in reduce: (acc, item) not (item, acc)
Tried to use forEach like map without external array + push
Task 1.2 - filter + reduce
Findings:
filter creates subset array, reduce aggregates subset
Chaining: .filter().reduce() works because filter returns array
Changing conditions shows different outputs, proves reusability
Errors made:
Chained wrong: reduce then filter (reduce returns non-array)
Forgot filter returns new array, thought it modified original
reduce callback used wrong property access after filter changed structure
Task 1.3 - Custom Array Method
Findings:
Simulating map: create result array, loop with index, push callback results
Must accept callback and optional thisArg like real methods
Callback receives (element, index, array) in that order
Real methods call callback for every index including sparse array holes
Errors made:
Used native map inside custom map (cheating, not simulating)
Forgot to handle sparse arrays (holes), used for..in instead of for loop
Didn't verify callback execution count with logs
Section 2: JavaScript Behind the Scenes
Task 2.1 - Hoisting
Findings:
var declarations hoist to top, initialize as undefined
let/const hoist but stay in temporal dead zone until declaration line
function declarations hoist fully, callable before definition
function expressions (var/let/const) don't hoist the assignment, only variable
Errors made:
Thought let hoisted same as var, got ReferenceError
Expected arrow function to hoist like function declaration
Tried to catch let TDZ error with try/catch at runtime (fails, it's parse time)
Task 2.2 - Call Stack
Findings:
Synchronous code: LIFO stack, inner function completes before outer resumes
setTimeout schedules callback, doesn't execute immediately
Event loop moves callbacks to stack after current execution clears
Async operations break linear execution order
Errors made:
Expected setTimeout callback to return value to outer function
Thought async code blocked execution, didn't understand event loop
Call stack logs showed entry but no exit logs (forgot to log before return)
Task 2.3 - Closure
Findings:
Inner function retains access to outer scope even after outer function returns
Closure captures variable reference, not value snapshot
Multiple closures from same outer function share same scope
Can modify closed-over variables if exposed via returned methods
Errors made:
Called inner function immediately inside outer, didn't return it
Thought closure captured copy of value, didn't see updates to outer variable
Forgot to test calling closure after outer variable changed
Section 3: Destructuring, Rest & Spread
Task 3.1 - Destructuring with Defaults
Findings:
Default values apply only when property is undefined, not null/0/""
Can rename variables: { oldName: newName } = obj
Can nest: { address: { city } } = obj
Rest pattern collects remaining: { a, ...rest } = obj
Errors made:
Expected default to apply for empty string "", only works for undefined
Forgot to declare const/let before destructuring assignment
Tried to destructure null/undefined, got TypeError
Task 3.2 - Rest Operator
Findings:
...args collects all remaining arguments into real array
Can use array methods on args (map, filter, reduce)
Replaces arguments object, more explicit and flexible
Must be last parameter in function definition
Errors made:
Used rest in middle of parameters (syntax error)
Forgot rest creates array, tried to use as single value
Named rest parameter poorly (args vs foods), confused self
Task 3.3 - Spread & References
Findings:
Spread {...obj} or [...arr] creates shallow copy only
Top-level primitives are copied (isolated)
Nested objects/arrays still shared references between original and copy
Deep copy requires JSON.parse(JSON.stringify()) or structuredClone
Reference assignment (=) shares everything, no isolation
Errors made:
Thought spread was deep copy, nested changes propagated unexpectedly
Used = instead of spread, both variables mutated together
Didn't test nested mutation, missed shallow copy limitation
JSON.stringify method lost functions and undefined values in object
